## Appendix — the ARM-LEVEL confirmations the plan names

The 'first RED line' quoted per block is whatever vitest printed first, which
is not necessarily the arm the neuter was aimed at. These are the aimed-at
arms, grepped out of the same captured runs:

N2, vitest anti-vacuity arm (d) — expected to fail, and did:
  × walks a real migration from clean, to flagged, to annotated, to un-pinned 1ms

N2, the self-test arm — expected to fail, and did:
  × the self-test passes over the committed fixtures 1ms

N3, vitest MEASURE_FAIL arm (f) — expected to fail, and did (it was the ONLY
failure of the 28, which is what makes N3 a targeted neuter rather than a
general breakage):
  × MEASURE_FAILs on an empty corpus instead of reporting a clean tree 3ms

N1 fired 7 SELF-TEST FAIL lines rather than 1: with the detector dead, every
annotated fixture also goes RED on its count (a header pinning 2 over a file
the engine now reads as holding 0). The line that matters is the first one
quoted in the N1 block — the red fixture whose whole job is to fire, not
firing.
